File: force-app/main/default/classes/FollowUpTaskService.cls
```apex
/**
 * Creates follow-up Tasks for the opportunities discussed in the weekly pipeline review.
 */
public with sharing class FollowUpTaskService {

    private static final String FOLLOW_UP_PREFIX = 'Follow up: ';
    private static final String FOLLOW_UP_STATUS = 'Not Started';
    private static final String FOLLOW_UP_PRIORITY = 'Normal';
    private static final Integer FOLLOW_UP_DUE_IN_DAYS = 7;

    /**
     * Creates one follow-up Task per opportunity.
     *
     * The Tasks are inserted with allOrNone = false, so a Task rejected by a Task
     * validation rule is reported individually and does not prevent the remaining
     * Tasks from being created.
     *
     * @param opportunityIds Ids of the opportunities that need a follow-up Task.
     * @return Ids of the opportunities for which no Task could be created.
     *         The set is empty when every Task was created.
     */
    public static Set<Id> createFollowUps(List<Id> opportunityIds) {
        Set<Id> failedOpportunityIds = new Set<Id>();

        if (opportunityIds == null || opportunityIds.isEmpty()) {
            return failedOpportunityIds;
        }

        // One Task per opportunity, even if an Id is passed more than once.
        List<Id> uniqueOpportunityIds = new List<Id>();
        Set<Id> collectedIds = new Set<Id>();
        for (Id opportunityId : opportunityIds) {
            if (opportunityId != null && collectedIds.add(opportunityId)) {
                uniqueOpportunityIds.add(opportunityId);
            }
        }

        Map<Id, Opportunity> opportunitiesById = new Map<Id, Opportunity>(
            [SELECT Id, Name, OwnerId
             FROM Opportunity
             WHERE Id IN :uniqueOpportunityIds]
        );

        Date dueDate = Date.today().addDays(FOLLOW_UP_DUE_IN_DAYS);
        List<Task> followUps = new List<Task>();

        for (Id opportunityId : uniqueOpportunityIds) {
            Opportunity opportunity = opportunitiesById.get(opportunityId);
            if (opportunity == null) {
                // Deleted, or not visible to the running user: no Task can be created.
                failedOpportunityIds.add(opportunityId);
                continue;
            }
            followUps.add(new Task(
                WhatId = opportunity.Id,
                OwnerId = opportunity.OwnerId,
                Subject = FOLLOW_UP_PREFIX + opportunity.Name,
                ActivityDate = dueDate,
                Status = FOLLOW_UP_STATUS,
                Priority = FOLLOW_UP_PRIORITY
            ));
        }

        if (followUps.isEmpty()) {
            return failedOpportunityIds;
        }

        // One DML statement for the whole review. The review can cover up to 250
        // opportunities, which would exceed the 150 DML statement governor limit if
        // every Task were inserted separately. allOrNone = false inserts every Task
        // that passes validation and reports a per-Task error for the rest.
        List<Database.SaveResult> results = Database.insert(followUps, false);
        for (Integer i = 0; i < results.size(); i++) {
            if (!results[i].isSuccess()) {
                // followUps[i].WhatId is the opportunity this Task was created for.
                failedOpportunityIds.add(followUps[i].WhatId);
            }
        }

        return failedOpportunityIds;
    }
}
```

What I did differently and why

- **No per-Task `insert` in a try/catch.** The review passes 150–250 opportunities in one transaction. Inserting one Task per loop iteration is 150–250 DML statements, which blows the 150 DML statement governor limit (a `LimitException` that cannot be caught). Instead: one SOQL query and one `Database.insert(followUps, false)` — 1 SOQL query and 1 DML statement regardless of volume.
- **`allOrNone = false` gives exactly the semantics you asked for.** Each Task is evaluated independently: a Task rejected by a compliance validation rule (or by ownership/field-level errors) comes back as a failed `SaveResult` and is not committed, while every other Task in the same call is committed. The failed `SaveResult`s are mapped back to their opportunity through `followUps[i].WhatId`, and those opportunity Ids are returned — the same contract as the requested method (empty set when everything succeeded).
- **Bulk query instead of a query per opportunity** (`WHERE Id IN :uniqueOpportunityIds`) to stay well within SOQL limits.
- **Small robustness choices:** duplicate Ids are collapsed so one opportunity gets exactly one follow-up; `null` entries are ignored; an Id that cannot be queried (deleted, or not visible to the running user) is reported as a failure, since no Task could be created for it. The class is `with sharing` so it only acts on opportunities the running user can see.
- **Known trade-off:** `Database.insert(list, false)` isolates *record-level* errors (validation rules — your compliance case). An unhandled Apex exception thrown by Task automation would still fail the whole statement; if hard isolation of that case were ever required, the fallback would be per-record inserts guarded by try/catch, with a lower batch size to respect DML limits. For the described requirement the bulk insert is the correct, bulk-safe choice.

