```apex
/**
 * Matches imported Leads to existing Contacts by email address.
 *
 * Pure in-memory matching: no SOQL and no DML. Emails are compared trimmed and
 * lower-cased, so matching ignores case and leading/trailing whitespace. A Lead
 * or Contact with a blank email never matches. When several Contacts share a
 * Lead's email, the first one in the supplied contacts order wins.
 */
public with sharing class LeadContactMatcher {

    /**
     * Normalizes an email for comparison.
     *
     * @return The trimmed, lower-cased email, or null when the email is null or
     *         blank (whitespace only), which must never match.
     */
    private static String normalizeEmail(String email) {
        if (String.isBlank(email)) {
            return null;
        }
        return email.trim().toLowerCase();
    }

    /**
     * Finds the Contact for each Lead by email address.
     *
     * @param leads    The Leads to look up. The result has the same size and
     *                 order as this list.
     * @param contacts The candidate Contacts, in priority order: on duplicate
     *                 emails the first occurrence in this list wins.
     * @return The matching Contact Id for each Lead, or null where there is no
     *         match (blank Lead email, or no Contact with that email).
     */
    public static List<Id> matchByEmail(List<Lead> leads, List<Contact> contacts) {
        List<Id> matches = new List<Id>();
        if (leads == null) {
            return matches;
        }

        // Index the Contacts once. containsKey() keeps the first Contact seen
        // for an email, which preserves the contacts order on duplicates.
        Map<String, Id> contactIdByEmail = new Map<String, Id>();
        if (contacts != null) {
            for (Contact c : contacts) {
                if (c == null) {
                    continue;
                }
                String key = normalizeEmail(c.Email);
                if (key != null && !contactIdByEmail.containsKey(key)) {
                    contactIdByEmail.put(key, c.Id);
                }
            }
        }

        // One entry per Lead, in the same order, null where nothing matches.
        for (Lead l : leads) {
            String key = (l == null) ? null : normalizeEmail(l.Email);
            Id matchId = null;
            if (key != null) {
                matchId = contactIdByEmail.get(key);
            }
            matches.add(matchId);
        }

        return matches;
    }
}
```

Note: I'd do one thing differently — I used a single-pass map instead of the nested loop. Behavior is identical (blank emails never match, and `containsKey` keeps the first Contact in `contacts` order on duplicates), but the nested loop is 3,000 × 3,000 = 9M iterations with a trim/normalize on each, which is a real CPU-time risk inside the import transaction; the map is ~6,000 operations total. Still no queries or DML, and `normalizeEmail` keeps the matching rules in one place.

