/**
 * Creates the CSAT survey Task for Case(s) that have just been closed.
 *
 * Runs as a Queueable so the survey step can evolve (e.g. a vendor integration)
 * without slowing down or breaking the Case closure itself. CaseTrigger also
 * uses the bulk constructor when more Cases are closed in one transaction than
 * the queueable job limit allows.
 */
public with sharing class CaseSurveyJob implements Queueable {

    private static final String SUBJECT_PREFIX = 'CSAT survey: ';
    private static final String TASK_STATUS = 'Not Started';
    private static final Integer DUE_IN_DAYS = 2;

    private final List<Id> caseIds;

    /** Survey a single Case (the normal path: one job per Case). */
    public CaseSurveyJob(Id caseId) {
        this.caseIds = new List<Id>{ caseId };
    }

    /** Survey several Cases in one job (bulk path, see CaseTrigger). */
    public CaseSurveyJob(List<Id> caseIds) {
        this.caseIds = (caseIds == null) ? new List<Id>() : new List<Id>(caseIds);
    }

    public void execute(QueueableContext context) {
        if (caseIds.isEmpty()) {
            return;
        }

        // Re-read the Cases: the data may have changed since the trigger ran.
        // Cases without a contact get no task.
        List<Task> surveyTasks = new List<Task>();
        Date dueDate = Date.today().addDays(DUE_IN_DAYS);

        for (Case c : [
            SELECT Id, CaseNumber, ContactId
            FROM Case
            WHERE Id IN :caseIds
            AND ContactId != null
        ]) {
            surveyTasks.add(
                new Task(
                    WhatId = c.Id,
                    WhoId = c.ContactId,
                    Subject = SUBJECT_PREFIX + c.CaseNumber,
                    ActivityDate = dueDate,
                    Status = TASK_STATUS
                )
            );
        }

        if (surveyTasks.isEmpty()) {
            return;
        }

        // Partial success: one invalid record must not block the others.
        List<Database.SaveResult> results = Database.insert(surveyTasks, false);
        for (Integer i = 0; i < results.size(); i++) {
            if (results[i].isSuccess()) {
                continue;
            }
            for (Database.Error err : results[i].getErrors()) {
                System.debug(
                    LoggingLevel.ERROR,
                    'CaseSurveyJob: no survey task for case ' + surveyTasks[i].WhatId +
                        ' - ' + String.valueOf(err.getStatusCode()) + ': ' + err.getMessage()
                );
            }
        }
    }
}
```

Explanations (what I changed and why)

1. **Trigger filtering** — no SOQL or DML in the loop; `Trigger.new`/`Trigger.oldMap` already contain everything needed (`IsClosed`, `ContactId`), so the trigger is bulk-safe for a 200-record transaction. Only the `!old.IsClosed && new.IsClosed` transition with a contact enqueues work, so later edits to closed cases do not enqueue anything.

2. **Governor limit (the one real deviation from "one job per case always")** — Apex allows a maximum of 50 queueable jobs per transaction (`Limits.getLimitQueueableJobs()`). Enqueueing one job per case for the nightly run's 200 cases would throw an uncatchable `System.LimitException` at the 51st `enqueueJob()` and roll back the entire case-closure transaction. So the trigger enqueues exactly one job per case (`System.enqueueJob(new CaseSurveyJob(c.Id))`) whenever the cases fit in the remaining job slots (typical list-view closes), and only falls back to a single bulk `CaseSurveyJob(List<Id>)` when they do not, keeping the transaction inside the limit while never dropping work. If all job slots were already consumed by other code in the same transaction, surveys are skipped with a warning rather than failing the case updates.

3. **Queueable** — re-queries the cases at execution time (the contact may have been removed between enqueue and execution) and skips contact-less cases, so "no contact = no task" holds regardless of timing. Tasks are inserted with `Database.insert(..., false)` so one bad record cannot block the others; failures are logged (swap `System.debug` for your org's error log / platform event if you want alerting). `ActivityDate` is `Date.today().addDays(2)` evaluated when the job runs.

4. **Security / style** — `with sharing`, named constants for the subject prefix, due offset and status, standard Task defaults (owner, priority) left untouched.

5. **Deliberately not added** — no duplicate suppression (a case reopened and re-closed should get a new survey) and no `Database.AllowsCallouts` yet; add it plus a Named Credential when the vendor integration lands so callouts remain authenticated and testable. A test class was not requested, but should cover the transition filter, no-contact cases and both enqueue branches.

